<h1>Michael A. Forti</h1>
<p>Judge up for retention, on the Chicago ballot November 3, 2026. Incumbent.</p>
<p>Circuit Court of Cook County</p>
<h2>Ratings</h2>
<ul>
<li>Chicago Bar Association: <a href="https://www.chicagobar.org/votejudges#:~:text=Michael%20A.%20Forti" rel="nofollow noopener">Qualified</a></li>
<li>Illinois State Bar Association: <a href="https://www.isba.org/judicial-elections/counties/cook#:~:text=Michael%20A.%20Forti" rel="nofollow noopener">Yes</a></li>
</ul>
<h2>Record</h2>
<p>Appointed to the Circuit Court in 2016 and again in 2018 after losing an election, selected as an associate judge in 2019, and elected a circuit judge in 2020; hears cases in the Domestic Relations Division. Injustice Watch reports that in 2022 he held Steve Fanady in civil contempt and ordered him jailed for failing to turn over $10 million in stock awarded to his ex-wife in their divorce; Fanady says he does not own the stock, and in 2025 the appellate court affirmed Judge Forti. He told Injustice Watch, &quot;I followed the law and I was affirmed.&quot; A judge stays on the bench with a yes from 60 percent of voters.</p>
<h2>Background</h2>
<p>Chief counsel and ethics officer at the Illinois Department of Transportation (2012-2015); earlier an attorney in the City of Chicago Law Department, rising to deputy corporation counsel (1994-2012), and an equity partner at Bell, Boyd &amp; Lloyd (1980-1994). Law degree from Northwestern University (1980).</p>
